 2. French doom loop warning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 City hall in Limoges, France, burns on Saturday. Photo: Pascal Lachenaud/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 This weekend brought violence in the streets of France. Today brought violence in its bond market. And therein lies a warning for other rich countries staring at big fiscal imbalances.

 Driving the news: There were outbursts of violence and looting in several French cities, following demonstrations over school quality and cost of living. They included rioters setting fire to the city hall in Limoges.

 The right-wing National Rally party seized on the events, positioning itself as the party best poised to restore order in elections next year. The events drove a new surge in French borrowing costs, with the benchmark 10-year French government bond yield rising 0.15 percentage point today, to 4.93%, flirting with new multidecade highs. The big picture: The events show the risk of a doom loop among fiscal policy, politics and markets — one that looks particularly ominous in France at the moment but that could equally apply to other advanced economies running large deficits, including the United States and Britain.

 Persistent deficits and rising interest rates compel fiscal austerity, but austerity — especially if it involves cutting pension and other public benefits — is deeply unpopular. This toxic unpopularity can cause people to take to the streets, and to elect leaders who promise to maintain benefit levels and defy the fiscal math. This generates bond market blowback in the form of higher rates, which makes reining in deficits all the more urgent. Rinse and repeat. The bottom line: The technocratic challenge of putting major advanced democracies on more sustainable fiscal footing is difficult. But managing the politics is looking downright impossible.

 
 
